# Bellwether extension shape 🐏🔔

## Decision

Bellwether (`@joelhooks/pi-bellwether`) is a generic Pi package for Herdr runtime management. It owns Herdr agent/pane commands and tools. Product-specific loop bridges should call or adapt Bellwether; they should not bury generic Herdr control inside loop-specific code.

## Initial scope

Commands:

- `/herdr-status`
- `/herdr-agents`
- `/herdr-start <name> -- <cmd>`
- `/herdr-send <target> <msg>`
- `/herdr-submit <target>`
- `/herdr-read <target>`
- `/herdr-focus <target>`
- `/herdr-stop <target>`

Tools:

- `herdr_status`
- `herdr_list_agents`
- `herdr_start_agent`
- `herdr_send_message`
- `herdr_submit`
- `herdr_read_agent`
- `herdr_focus_agent`
- `herdr_stop_agent`

## Design receipts

- Pi package docs say package resources belong under `package.json` `pi` manifest or conventional directories, and package imports of Pi core packages should be peer dependencies.
- Pi extension docs say use `pi.registerTool()` and `pi.registerCommand()`; tool results need cloneable `details`; optional runtime dependencies should be lazy.
- Herdr CLI 0.7.0 exposes `herdr agent list|get|read|send|focus|start`, `herdr pane send-keys|close|list`, and `herdr status`.
- Current machine receipt while building: client `herdr 0.7.0`, server `0.6.8`, protocol mismatch reported by `herdr status`, but agent/pane list/read commands still returned JSON.

## Boundaries

- This package starts no background process at Pi startup.
- Herdr binary lookup happens lazily when a command/tool runs.
- Stop/close requires confirmation in slash command and `confirm: true` in the LLM tool.
- Send and submit are intentionally separate so agents do not accidentally execute text while composing.
